---
import { site } from '../config';
import LegalLayout from '../layouts/LegalLayout.astro';
---

<LegalLayout
  title="About"
  eyebrow="About the project"
  description="Who makes torwache, what the extension does, and the principles that define its scope."
>
  <h1>About torwache</h1>

  <p>
    torwache is a small Chrome extension for one specific job: keeping websites on a local
    blocklist from opening. Add a domain, decide whether its subdomains belong to the same rule,
    and switch the entry on. Chrome enforces the active rules in its own network stack. There is
    no account, hosted dashboard, cloud sync, advertising, behavioural telemetry, focus score, or
    paid tier inside the extension.
  </p>

  <h2>Why it exists</h2>
  <p>
    Many website blockers have grown into broad productivity systems with schedules, timers,
    cross-device services, analytics, and subscription plans. Those products can be useful, but
    they solve a larger problem. torwache is for someone who already knows which domains should
    stop opening and wants the browser to enforce that decision without adding another service to
    their life.
  </p>

  <h2>Who is responsible</h2>
  <p>
    torwache is developed by {site.legal.representedBy} and published by {site.legal.operatorName},
    a company registered in Germany. The provider address, commercial register entry, VAT number,
    and legally responsible person are listed in the <a href="/imprint/">imprint</a>. Questions
    that need a personal reply can be sent through the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>How trust is earned</h2>
  <p>
    The source code is public under the Zero-Clause BSD licence. The repository includes strict
    TypeScript, automated tests, the WXT build configuration, Chrome permission declarations, and
    the code that stores and validates domains. Releases are built from that repository. This
    makes claims about permissions, local storage, and network behaviour inspectable rather than
    dependent on marketing copy.
  </p>

  <h2>What remains separate</h2>
  <p>
    The extension and this website are separate technical surfaces. The extension makes no
    analytics requests. torwache.com uses Fathom Analytics for aggregate website page views, as
    described in the <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a>. Visiting the website is not required
    for the installed extension to block domains.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
